## Goal

> Prove the core architecture end to end: an athlete can log a single lift (exercise, weight, reps) on the Android app and see it saved and displayed back, reliably, on a real or emulated device.

## Why This Goal

This is a 2-person, 16-week capstone. Before building the data-science layer (e1RM, volume, overtraining detection) or any coach-facing feature, the team needs proof that the basic log → save → display pipeline works.

A thin, demoable slice also gives the team something to show at the earliest sprint review, rather than partial work across many features.

## Documented Change to the Goal

During Sprint 1 planning and execution, the team descoped two pieces of work:

- **User authentication:** moved to Sprint 2.
- **Backend API:** replaced with local-only persistence (Room database on-device) and moved to Sprint 2.

This let the log → save → display goal still be demoed end to end within the 2-week window. The Sprint Goal itself (a working local log-and-view flow) did not change, only the implementation approach used to reach it.
